SECTION A: MIGRATIONS 0001-0014 (Shared Base)
================================================================================

Identity Status:
  Sandbox baseline (634ec66) vs closest match (0d73697): ALL 14 IDENTICAL ✅
  Sandbox baseline (634ec66) vs origin HEAD (b1082d7):   13 DIFFER, 1 identical
  
  0001-0013: ALL DIFFER between sandbox and origin HEAD
  0014:      IDENTICAL between sandbox and origin HEAD

Nature of 0001-0013 Differences:
  The differences were introduced by two origin commits AFTER the v5.1.0 merge:
  
  Commit 6ee4229: "fix(migrations): hybrid ENUM fix - idempotent CREATE TYPE +
                   postgresql.ENUM"
    Changed 11 files (0001-0009, 0012, 0013)
    +155 / -78 lines
    
  Commit dfd1947: "fix: hybrid ENUM migration fix v5.1.2"
    Changed 3 files (0001, 0010, 0011)
    +45 / -39 lines

  The changes fall into TWO categories:

  CATEGORY 1: ENUM Type Creation Syntax (cosmetic/behavioral)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Sandbox (v5.1.0):  Idempotent DO/EXCEPTION blocks
      DO $$ BEGIN
        CREATE TYPE x AS ENUM (...);
      EXCEPTION WHEN duplicate_object THEN NULL;
      END $$
    
    Origin HEAD:        Direct CREATE TYPE (non-idempotent)
      CREATE TYPE x AS ENUM (...)
    
    Impact: Both produce the same database schema on a CLEAN database.
    The sandbox version is MORE ROBUST for re-runs (handles pre-existing types).
    The origin version is SIMPLER but will fail if ENUMs already exist.

  CATEGORY 2: ENUM Column Type Usage (sa.Enum → postgresql.ENUM)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Sandbox (v5.1.0):  sa.Enum("value1", "value2", ...)
    Origin HEAD:        postgresql.ENUM("value1", "value2", ..., create_type=False)
    
    Affected: ~10 ENUM columns across multiple tables
    Impact: Same database schema produced. The postgresql.ENUM with
    create_type=False is more explicit about PG-specific handling.
    
  CATEGORY 3: ONE ACTUAL SCHEMA DIFFERENCE in 0001
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Sandbox:  sa.Column("prompt_id", sa.String(36), ...)
    Origin:   sa.Column("prompt_id", UUID(as_uuid=True), ...)
    
    Impact: Different column type for prompt_id in storyboard_scenes.
    Sandbox uses String(36) (VARCHAR), Origin uses native UUID.
    This is a REAL schema divergence — not just syntax.

  Column count in 0001: IDENTICAL (103 columns in both versions)
  Table count in 0001: IDENTICAL (12 tables in both versions)

